# The influence of transient strain-rate deformation conditions on the deformed microstructure of aluminium alloy Al–1% Mg

G. J. BAXTER{1, T. $\mathbf{FURU}_{\ast}^{+2}$ , Q. ZHU}2, J. A. WHITEMAN1} and C. M. SELLARS1 1Department of Engineering Materials, University of Sheeld, Mappin Street, Sheeld S1 3JD, U.K. and 2Department of Engineering, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, U.K.
(Received 4 January 1999; accepted 13 April 1999)
AbstractÐConstant and varying strain-rate deformation conditions have been applied to an $4l{-}1\%$ $\mathbf{Mg}$ alloy using plane strain compression (PSC) testing. Transmission electron microscope (TEM) investigations have shown signi®cant variations in internal microstructural parameters after dierent strain-rate histories to a strain of 1.0, even though the ®nal ¯ow stress was the same. Detailed investigations have been carried out in the transient regime, and after further deformation at constant strain rate. Considerable attention is given to establishing the errors associated with the experimental observations. The observations illustrate the complexity of the evolution of microstructure with strain. The relevance of the measurements to modelling the evolution of the microstructure is discussed. $\copyright$ 1999 Acta Metallurgica Inc. Published by Elsevier Science Ltd. All rights reserved.
## 1. INTRODUCTION
Approximately $15\%$ of the world's primary aluminium production is used each year in the manufacture of cans for the beverage industry. With increasing demands from can manufacturers to lower production costs and increase properties, there is a growing need for an ever more detailed understanding and control of the microstructural changes which occur during each stage of can production, particularly during thermomechanical processing [1]. It is widely recognized that having soundly based physical models of microstructural evolution during thermomechanical processing is highly desirable if truly predictive capabilities in relation to the eect of process variables on subsequent processing and product properties are to be achieved [2]. The bene®ts of having such predictive capabilities in the aluminium industry are clear [3].
During industrial hot working operations of forging, rolling or extrusion the strain rate varies during the deformation process and heat transfer leads to the development of temperature gradients and changes in the mean temperature of the workpiece. In the case of rolling and forging, sequential passes may also take place at dierent temperatures and strain rates. By contrast, laboratory tests are usually carried out under constant deformation conditions to obtain relationships between ¯ow stress and the strain, strain rate and temperature of deformation [4, 5]. From such tests, constitutive relationships have been proposed [6±9] and have been used to derive working forces for the hot working operations by considering the mean strain rate and temperature during a pass [10±12]. Present models, however, have no predictive power outside their experimentally established range of application. They cannot be applied to the complex temperature and strain-rate transients encountered during thermomechanical processing. In order to progress further, an improved knowledge of the link between deformation structures and recrystallization kinetics is required, together with a better understanding of the evolution of microstructure during transient deformation conditions [13].
The key variables that describe a deformed structure are: (i) the subgrain size; (ii) the misorientation between neighbouring subgrains; and (iii) the internal dislocation density of the subgrains. These in¯uence ¯ow stress and stored energy. The microstructural evolution during cold deformation of metals and alloys has been the subject of many studies over the last three decades [14±21]. Fewer studies have investigated the microstructural evolution in aluminium alloys at high deformation temperatures [14, 22, 23].
During thermomechanical processing, the recrystallization behaviour of a material is in¯uenced by the chemical composition and the strain, strain rate and temperature of deformation. The latter parameters can, to some extent, be controlled during processing but rarely, if ever, are constant deformation conditions produced. Current models rely on data which have been obtained from laboratory experiments carried out under nominally constant deformation conditions, and can thus only accurately model these constant deformation conditions.
It is necessary therefore to start investigations from model alloys and to carry out controlled laboratory experiments. The tests must involve simple deformation paths that reproduce the main characteristics of hot rolling, and must be performed at both constant and varying strain rates. The alloy chosen for these investigations is a relatively \`\`simple'' (non can stock), stable particle containing, aluminium alloy in which the eects of precipitation are small. The data presented in this paper provide a basis for the development of physically based internal state variable models for dynamic ¯ow stress and subsequent static recrystallization.
## 2. EXPERIMENTAL PROCEDURE
The material used in the present work is an Al± $1\%$ $\mathbf{Mg}$ alloy produced for a BRITE/EURAM project relating microstructure and texture evolution in hot working [2]. The composition is given in Table 1.
Plane strain compression (PSC) specimens of $60.0\times50.0\times10.00\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ were machined from homogenized, rolled and annealed material with fully recrystallized grains of major intercept length $(117\pm5)\mu\mathrm{m}$ . The PSC tests were carried out on a computer controlled servohydraulic machine, as described elsewhere [24±27]. A graphite lubricant (Aicheson DAG EG1403) was used for all test specimens. The pre-test furnace temperature was held at $650^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ which enabled all the specimens to be brought to the test temperature of $385^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at a constant heating rate of $2^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{s}$ . When the specimens reached the test temperature, they were transferred to the testing furnace and held for $3\mathrm{min}$ to equilibrate at this temperature prior to deformation. The specimens were quenched within $1.5\mathrm{s}$ of the end of deformation. In some tests, specimens were fully recrystallized in situ by holding them in the test furnace for $10\mathrm{min}$ after the end of deformation before quenching.
An initial series of tests was carried out in which specimens were deformed to a strain of 1.0 with the strain-rate histories shown in Fig. 1(a); constant strain rates of (i) $0.25/\mathrm{s}$ , (ii) $2.5/\mathrm{s}$ and (iii) $25/\mathrm{s}$ , (iv) a logarithmically increasing strain rate from 0.25 to $2.5/\mathrm{s}$ , (v) a logarithmically decreasing strain rate from 25 to $2.5/\mathrm{s}$ . Tests were also carried out in which the same strain-rate paths were followed but the tests were terminated at strains of 0.4 and 0.7. Additional tests were performed in which further straining (beyond 1.0) at the constant strain rate $(2.5/\mathrm{s})$ was applied to specimens given strain-rate histories (iv) and (v).
Reproducible stress±strain curves were derived from the load±displacement data as described elsewhere [24] and are shown in Fig. 1(b). It is clear from the curves that the ®nal ¯ow stress is essentially the same $(60\pm2\mathrm{MPa})$ after constant $(2.5/\mathrm{s})$ , increasing and decreasing strain-rate histories. The as-deformed specimens were characterized by optical microscopy and thin foil transmission electron microscopy (TEM) of specimens prepared in the transverse direction, in the RD/ND plane, using a standard method described elsewhere [27]. Care was taken to remove the foils from the region of PSC specimens in which the deformation was nearly homogeneous, away from the dead zone and edges of the deformed zone. For each condition analysed: (i) the average subgrain size, d, was measured by the circle intercept method using at least 150 subgrains; (ii) accurate three-dimensional misorientations, $\bar{\theta}$ , between adjacent subgrains were determined by TEM using the average of between 150 and 240 measurements across boundaries in several foils. A few measurements of misorientation were also made using the electron back-scattering pattern (EBSP) technique [28]. (iii) The dislocation density inside subgrains, $\rho_{\mathrm{i}}$ , was determined by the method described by Castro-Fernandez and Sellars [29]. The local foil thickness was calculated from at least ten subgrains using the two-beam dynamical theory of convergent beam electron diraction [30]. For these determinations $\{220\}_{\mathrm{Al}}$ re¯ections were taken at an accelerating voltage of $100\mathrm{kV}$ with an extinction distance, $\xi_{\mathrm{g}}$ , of close to $100\mathrm{nm}$ .
Errors in measurements were minimized by measuring a large number of subgrains and making measurements in more than one foil for each condition. The subgrain size measurements were always taken at near to $0^{\circ}$ tilt in the TEM to avoid any distortions due to sectioning. The circle intercept method was used in all cases to avoid errors arising from aspect ratio. The $95\%$ con®dence limits were determined to be no more than $\pm12\%$ of average subgrain size in each case, and error bars are shown on the ®gures.
In the measurement and comparison of misorientation between subgrains $(\bar{\theta})$ there are errors due to the accuracy with which $\bar{\theta}$ can be measured as well as errors due to the number of measurements made. Errors between foils were minimized by using the same procedure for each condition, by the same person, making a similar number of measurements in each case when more than one foil was used. The $95\%$ con®dence limits were determined for each condition. These were within the range from $\pm9\%$ to $\pm17\%$ with an average of $\pm11\%$ of $\bar{\theta}$ . Again error bars are shown in the ®gures.
In the present investigation the texture of the subgrains from which dislocation density measurements were made is not taken into account. Since the magnitude of any dierences resulting from texture dierences is small [31], this has little in¯uence on the results, particularly when compared with statistical errors in the measurements (which in this investigation are, on average, $\pm23\%$ ). In fact, after decreasing strain-rate deformation, the dislocation density in some local regions was so high that it could not be measured. The reported results in these cases thus also have some systematic error.
Measurements of the kinetics of recrystallization after the hot deformation described in this work have been made. The results and a discussion of the recrystallization process are detailed elsewhere [32].
## 3. RESULTS
As described previously [27, 33], a major feature of the deformed microstructures for all the present deformation conditions when examined optically is the occurrence of microbands within the grains.
These have a mean orientation of about $35^{\circ}$ to the \`\`rolling'' plane and are parallel to the transverse direction. These structures have been discussed in detail for deformation at $305^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [27] and in rather less detail for deformation at $385^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [32]. It has been shown that they correlate directly with bands of subgrain boundaries of high misorientation observed in the TEM, as illustrated, for example in Fig. 2. The boundary misorientations shown in the montage in Fig. 2(a) were measured individually and clearly show the microband boundaries, as illustrated by the thicker boundaries in the schematic in Fig. 2(b). These microband boundaries are less complex than the dense dislocation walls and microbands observed after room temperature deformation of aluminium alloys [34], but nevertheless indicate that the development of \`\`geometrically necessary'' boundaries arising from local lattice curvature, as well as the incidental dislocation boundaries is an important feature of the microstructure evolution. Typically, the curvature accumulates in one direction across two±three boundaries, and then reverses so that there is no long-range curvature.
In quantifying the boundary spacing and misorientation, no distinction was made between the dierent boundaries so that $\bar{\boldsymbol\delta}$ is the mean linear intercept boundary spacing and $\bar{\theta}$ the mean boundary misorientation, rather than the spacing and misorientation of the microbands only.
## 3.1. Strain of 1.0
Under constant strain-rate conditions, the ¯ow stress at a strain of 1.0 increases with increasing strain rate $\mathrm{50\MPa}$ at $0.25/\mathrm{s}$ , $63\mathrm{MPa}$ at $2.5/\mathrm{s}$ , $76\mathrm{MPa}$ at $25/\mathrm{s}$ ) as illustrated in Fig. 1(b). The TEM micrographs in Fig. 3 show a decrease in the average subgrain size, $\bar{\delta}$ , with increasing strain rate or ¯ow stress, $\sigma$ . The measured average subgrain sizes are 2.6, 1.5 and $1.0\mu\mathrm{m}$ at 0.25, 2.5 and $25/\mathrm{s}$ , respectively. From these results, and other results from tests carried out on the same alloy at dierent temperatures and strain rates [27], a linear relationship between the steady-state ¯ow stress, $\sigma$ , and $\bar{\boldsymbol\delta}^{-1}$ , is found.
Specimens which underwent increasing (0.25±2.5/ s) and decreasing $(25-2.5/\mathrm{s})$ transients in strain rate show a ¯ow stress at a strain of 1.0 which is virtually the same as that after a constant strain rate of $2.5/\mathrm{s}$ [Fig. 1(b)]. Figure 4 compares the microstructures obtained at a strain of 1.0 after (a) increasing and (b) decreasing strain-rate histories. The internal microstructural parameters measured for these conditions are compared in Table 2 with those for the constant strain-rate $(2.5/\mathrm{s})$ case. After an increasing strain rate, all the parameters are similar to those measured after the constant strainrate history. This suggests that microstructural changes during the increasing transient occur at a rate which is fast enough to match the change in strain rate. Signi®cantly dierent values of the microstructural parameters are measured in the decreasing strain-rate case, even though the ®nal ¯ow stress is close to that after constant strain rate [Fig. 1(b)]. In the decreasing strain-rate case $\rho_{\mathrm{i}}$ is a factor of 2±3 times higher, $\bar{\delta}$ is $\sim20\%$ lower and $\bar{\theta}$ is ${\sim}40\%$ lower than the corresponding values measured in the constant strain-rate case. It is of interest to establish the evolution of this microstructure with strain, and to determine the strain beyond 1.0 that is required for the decreasing strain-rate case to obtain a \`\`steady-state'' microstructure (i.e. a microstructure corresponding to that for constant strain-rate deformation).
## 3.2. Evolution of microstructure
Figure 5 compares the evolution of the measured internal variables with strain for the dierent strainrate histories. Lines are drawn through the points to illustrate the trends observed in the measurements. In the constant strain-rate case, $\bar{\boldsymbol\delta}$ reaches a steady-state value of ${\sim}1.5\mu\mathrm{m}$ by a strain of about 0.5. The mean misorientation $\bar{\theta}$ increases with strain and reaches a steady-state value of about $4^{\circ}$ at a strain of ${\sim}1.0$ . Due to the constraint of the specimen thickness becoming less than the diameter of the thin foil $(3\mathrm{mm})$ some measurements were made of the mean misorientation at higher strains using EBSD. These are shown with larger triangular symbols in Fig. 5(c) and the error bars on these measurements are shown. The internal dislocation density $\rho_{\mathrm{i}}$ increases with strain to a steady-state value of $2\times10^{13}/\mathrm{m}^{2}$ at a strain of approximately 0.5. These observations are in agreement with other work on similar alloys after constant strain-rate deformation [5, 31]. In the increasing strain-rate case, a re®nement in subgrain size is observed with strain. At a strain of 0.4 $\dot{\varepsilon}=0.63/{\mathrm{s}}$ ), $\bar{\delta}$ is $1.9\mu\mathrm{m}$ and at a strain of 0.7 $(\dot{\varepsilon}=1.26/\mathrm{s})$ , $\bar{\delta}$ is $1.6\mu\mathrm{m}$ . At a strain of 1.0, $\bar{\boldsymbol\delta}$ matches that of the constant strainrate case. From the limited data available, $\bar{\theta}$ follows a similar evolution with strain to 1.0 as in the constant strain-rate case. No data have been obtained in the present investigation for the evolution of $\rho_{\mathrm{i}}$ under increasing strain-rate conditions.
Of most interest in the present work is the evolution of the internal parameters in the decreasing strain-rate case, in which the measurements at a strain of 1.0 are signi®cantly dierent from those measured after constant strain rate. Specimens were analysed at strains of 0.4 $\dot{\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}}=10/\mathrm{s},$ ), 0.7 $\dot{\boldsymbol{\upvarepsilon}}=5/\mathrm{s})$ ), 1.0 $(\dot{\varepsilon}=2.5/s)$ ), 1.1 $(\dot{\varepsilon}=2.5/\mathrm{s})$ , 1.3 $(\dot{\varepsilon}=2.5/\mathrm{s}$ ) and 1.6 $(\dot{\varepsilon}=2.5/\mathrm{s})$ ), where the numbers in parentheses indicate the instantaneous strain rates. The subgrain size $\bar{\boldsymbol\delta}$ is $1.2\mu\mathrm{m}$ at a strain of 0.4 and similar values were measured at strains of 0.7 and 1.0. Beyond a strain of 1.0 a sharp increase in $\bar{\delta}$ is observed, so that by a strain of $1.1\ \bar{\delta}$ is still ${\sim}1.7\mu\mathrm{m}$ . This value is slightly higher than the corresponding steadystate value $(1.5\mu\mathrm{m})$ . At a strain of 1.3, $\bar{\boldsymbol\delta}$ is still ${\sim}1.7\mu\mathrm{m}$ and at a strain of 1.6, $\bar{\boldsymbol\delta}$ is $1.5\mu\mathrm{m}$ , close to the value after constant strain-rate deformation.
The average misorientation between subgrains, $\bar{\theta}$ , when the strain rate is decreased, increases gradually with strain but at a slower rate than under constant strain-rate conditions. At a strain of 1.0, $\bar{\theta}$ is about $60\%$ of that measured after constant strainrate deformation. On further straining beyond 1.0, $\bar{\theta}$ increases and reaches a value essentially the same as that after constant strain rate at a strain of around 1.6.
The internal dislocation density, $\rho_{\mathrm{i}}$ , increases signi®cantly during the decreasing strain-rate transient from a relatively low value of the order of $1\times10^{13}/$ ${\mathrm{m}}^{2}$ at a strain of 0.4 to over $4.5\times10^{13}/\mathrm{m}^{2}$ at a strain of 1.0 (a factor of at least 2.5 times higher than the constant strain-rate case). With further straining (beyond $\varepsilon=1.0\dot{}$ ) at $2.5/\mathrm{s}$ , $\rho_{\mathrm{i}}$ decreases towards the steady-state value. Between a strain of 1.0 and 1.3, randomly selected regions of foils had such high dislocation densities that they could not be measured. The experimental values are therefore low, as shown by the arrows in Fig. 5(a) with the dotted line indicating the best estimate of the true values.
Figure 6 compares the microstructures of the decreasing transient case at strains of 1.1 and 1.3 with that of the constant strain-rate case at a strain of 1.3. The higher $\rho_{\mathrm{i}}$ in the decreasing strain-rate case is clear from these micrographs because the foils are of similar thickness. At a strain of 1.6 the microstructures are essentially the same after the two dierent strain-rate histories.
## 4. DISCUSSION
The results presented in this paper are the outcome of a number of careful and time-consuming experiments. The uncertainties in the experimental measurements are considered here in relation to the evolution of the microstructural variables, and in an accompanying paper [32] in relation to a mathematical model of the recrystallization.
Measurements taken after constant strain-rate deformation at various temperatures show the stress $\sigma$ is proportional to the reciprocal of the subgrain size d. This observation is well established for constant strain-rate deformation as reported in earlier work on aluminium alloys [9] and ferritic stainless steel [35]. The observation that a similar ¯ow stress of $60\mathrm{MPa}$ was reached at a strain of 1.0 for specimens after constant or transient strain rate suggests that a mechanical equation of state applies, i.e. the ¯ow stress is controlled by the instantaneous values of strain rate and temperature (or Zener±Hollomon parameter, $Z)$ . The ¯ow stress may well follow a mechanical equation of state but, in order to model the behaviour using physically based parameters, the microstructural evolution coupled with this must be understood.
The measured microstructural parameters and their change during the transient of a reduction in strain rate are shown in Fig. 5. This ®gure shows that the relationship between the measured parameters is fairly simple in the constant strain-rate material. Above a strain of about 0.5 there is a roughly constant subgrain size and dislocation density with the main change being in the subgrain misorientation which increases up to a strain of 1.0 and then remains constant. A simple model that predicts this behaviour has been developed [36±38].
The subgrain size in the decreasing strain-rate situation is smaller than that produced by a constant strain rate at strains below about 1.0. There is then an abrupt increase in subgrain size until the size in the decreasing strain-rate case is greater than that at constant strain rate at a strain of 1.1. The subgrain size in the reducing strain-rate case then reduces to approach the constant strain-rate case with further increase in strain until they are the same after a strain of about 1.6. Although this behaviour is obtained from examination of the experimental values, the experimental uncertainty is such that above a strain of 1.0 the subgrain size may not be signi®cantly dierent in the two materials. There is no doubt that below a strain of 1.0 the subgrain size in the reducing strain-rate specimens is lower than that produced at constant strain rate. The sharp increase with strain between strains of 1.0 and 1.1 involves recovery and may therefore be more dependent on time than on strain. In this respect, it is noteworthy that the total time to a strain of 1.0 is $0.36\mathrm{~s~}$ and the time interval from a strain of $1.0–1.1$ is $0.40\mathrm{~s~}$ .
As shown in the accompanying paper [32] there is a proportionate increase in the recrystallized grain size corresponding to the sharp increase in subgrain size.
The misorientation between the subgrains in the reducing strain-rate case Fig. 5, increases more slowly than in the constant strain-rate case below a strain of 1.0. Above this value of strain the constant strain-rate material has a constant misorientation and the reducing strain-rate material eventually attains the same misorientation at a strain of about 1.6.
The other major change brought about by the transient in strain rate is in the dislocation density; above a strain of about 0.6 the dislocation density in the reducing strain-rate material is higher than in the material with a constant strain rate, in which the dislocation density remains essentially constant. The reducing strain-rate material goes through a maximum in dislocation density at a strain of 1.1 and reaches the density of the constant strain-rate material at a strain of about 1.6, i.e. after a strain of 0.6 at constant strain rate.
From the observations that the subgrain structure is in the form of microbands oriented at about $35^{\circ}$ to the \`\`rolling'' plane, with low misorientation subgrain boundaries within them, it is concluded that the higher misorientation boundaries in the bands are geometrically necessary boundaries to accommodate local lattice curvatures. If it is assumed that the local lattice curvature arises both from the misorientation across these boundaries and from excess density of dislocations of the same Burgers vector within subgrains (geometrically necessary dislocations), the local radius of curvature $R$ may be calculated simply by reference to Fig. 7.
Considering an area $A$ of matrix in which there are $N_{A}$ intersections per unit area of excess dislocations of a given Burgers vector $b$ leads to a curvature of radius $R_{\rho_{\mathrm{e}}}$ [see Fig. 7(a)]
$$
\Delta s=\Delta R_{\rho_{\mathrm{e}}}\phi=N_{A}A b
$$
where
$$
A=\phi R_{\rho_{\mathrm{e}}}\Delta R_{\rho_{\mathrm{e}}}
$$
hence
$$
{\frac{1}{R_{\rho_{\mathrm{e}}}}}=N_{A}b.
$$
Considering a subgrain (microband) boundary
between two subgrains of dimension $s$ [see Fig. 7(b)]
$$
\theta=\frac{b}{h}=b\bar{N}_{L}
$$
where $h=1/\bar{N}_{L}$ is the spacing of dislocations in the subgrain boundary
$$
\tan\mathrm{\frac{\theta}{2}}\cong\frac{\theta}{2}=\frac{s}{2}\frac{1}{R_{\rho_{\delta}}}.
$$
Hence
$$
\frac{1}{R_{\rho_{\delta}}}=\frac{\theta}{s}=\frac{b\bar{N}_{L}}{s}.
$$
In two dimensions, for subgrains and excess dislocations inside subgrains
$$
\frac{1}{R}=N_{A}b+\frac{\bar{N}_{L}b}{s}.
$$
Because the axes of curvature in rolling and plane strain compression lie in the transverse direction, the experimental observations made in the through thickness±longitudinal plane approximate to the two-dimensional situation of Fig. 7, so equation (5) may be rewritten as
$$
\frac{1}{R}=\rho_{\mathrm{e}}b+\rho_{\mathrm{b}}b
$$
where $\rho_{\mathrm{b}}$ is the dislocation density in the microband boundary. In the experimental measurements, $\bar{\theta}$ was determined for all subgrain boundaries as was the boundary spacing $\bar{\delta}$ , so equation (6) is rewritten as
$$
\frac{1}{R}\approx\rho_{\mathrm{e}}b+\frac{\bar{\theta}}{\bar{\delta}}.
$$
From other experimental observations on $\mathbf{Al-Mg} $ alloys, deformed under constant strain-rate conditions, in which the straining direction was reversed at a strain of 0.15 and the structures were compared with those after a unidirectional strain to 0.30 [39] it is estimated that $\begin{array}{r}{\rho_{\mathrm{e}}\approx\frac{1}{2}\rho_{\mathrm{i}}}\end{array}$ , where $\rho_{\mathrm{i}}$ is the internal dislocation density in the subgrains. However, when experimental values are substituted into equation (7) the second term dominates for constant strain-rate deformation, so in the following calculations for all the present deformation conditions it is assumed that $\rho_{\mathrm{e}}\approx\rho_{\mathrm{i}}$ . The resulting curvature is shown as a function of strain in Fig. 8. As seen in Fig. 8(a) the curvature initially increases almost linearly with strain to a steady-state value. It is easier to envisage this in terms of the decrease in radius of curvature to a steady-state value of ${\sim}22\mu\mathrm{m}$ , as shown in Fig. 8(b). The important feature of Fig. 8 is that within the experimental con®- dence limits all the data fall on a single curve, independent of the strain-rate history over the range of conditions studied. The points are from experimentally measured values substituted in equation (7), the arrows indicate how they are eected by the true, higher dislocation density.
The unique dependence of $R$ on strain has important implications during the microstructural transients during and after strain-rate change. To maintain the relationship, a change in one of the variables on the right-hand side of equation (7) requires that one or both of the other variables also change. The consequences of a subgrain (microband) boundary dissociating are illustrated schematically in Fig. 9. The initial structure is shown in Fig. 9(a), with dissociation of the central boundary occurring in Fig. 9(b) and resulting in ejection into the adjacent subgrains of the geometrically necessary dislocations to retain the curvature. This gives an increase in subgrain size, but a high internal dislocation density. These dislocations are of the same Burgers vector, so they cannot annihilate either mutually or to a signi®cant extent with dislocations in the \`\`random'' three-dimensional internal network. With increasing strain (time) these geometrically necessary dislocations can join the adjacent subgrain (microband) boundaries to increase their misorientation and so retain the overall local lattice curvature, Fig. 9(c). This schematic change in internal dislocation density is clearly consistent with the experimental observations after a decrease in strain rate, Figs 4 and 5, and emphasizes the critical role of the microbands and lattice curvatures in the present $\mathbf{Al-Mg} $ alloys. This important role of microbands is consistent with observations on the eects of reversing the direction of straining [39].
In contrast to the present observations, previous work on $4l-1\%$ $\mathbf{Mg}$ [40] and $\mathrm{Al-l\%}$ $M g-1\%$ Mn alloys [29] after a strain-rate change indicated that the transient strain (time) interval was shorter for the internal dislocation density than for the subgrain size. In these investigations, microbands were not a signi®cant feature of the observed microstructures, so that they may be considered to comprise only the \`\`random'' three-dimensional dislocation network and the statistically incidental dislocation boundaries, without signi®cant local lattice curvature. The reason for the dierence from the present alloy behaviour is not understood, but recent observations indicate that purity of the alloys may be important [41]. However, it is tentatively proposed that the dierent behaviour during and after transients in strain rate indicate that the strain (time) intervals for the microstructural variables increase as: \`\`random'' dislocation density $<$ < subgrain size/ microband spacing $\mathit{\Theta}<$ geometrically necessary dislocation density/microband misorientation. Clearly, further research is required to support this conclusion and to test the interpretation on other alloy systems subjected to transient strain-rate deformation.
## 5. CONCLUSIONS
The interaction between the microstructural variables subgrain size, subgrain misorientation and dislocation density within the subgrains has been examined for an aluminium± $1\%$ magnesium alloy deformed in plane strain compression. The microstructural features have been quantitatively measured in thin foils. A key feature is the development of microbands at about $35^{\circ}$ to the \`\`rolling' plane.
For deformation at constant strain rate, the subgrain size decreases with strain until a steady-state size is reached. Associated with this, the misorientation between microbands and subgrains increases with strain to a steady-state value. The dislocation density within the grains also increases with strain until a steady-state value is reached.
Under transient conditions of a reducing strain rate to a strain of 1.0, the subgrain size and misorientation are smaller than in steady state. During deformation at constant strain rate after the transient, the subgrains grow by a process of dissociation of subgrain boundaries, which produces a larger number of dislocations in the subgrains until the values characteristic of steady state are eventually achieved at a strain of about 1.6.
It is shown that if local lattice curvature is considered to arise both from misorientation across the microband boundaries and from geometrically necessary dislocations within subgrains, the radius of curvature decreases with increasing strain to a steady-state value under both constant and transient strain-rate conditions. This provides a quantitative interpretation of the observed transients of the individual microstructural features.
